#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167896} :activity/id #uuid "797cab7f-b62f-590f-9f20-4093fac9d314" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167991 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of ordinary differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) =\nA\n\\mathbf{x}(t) +\n\\mathbf{f}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix and $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is a forcing vector.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll derive the formula for the particular solution of the above system using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_c(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{c}$ is a vector of constants, and $\\Phi$ is the fundamental matrix.\n\nAs usual, for the method of variation of parameters, we'll look for a particular solution of our inhomogeneous system by varying the constant parameters, i.e., in the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t)  = \\Phi \\, \\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined.\n\nDifferentiating $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φ\\,u(t))^{′} \\\\  & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{x}_p(t)$ is a solution, substituting it back into the initial system gives a true equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\Phi$ is a fundamental matrix, $\\Phi' = A \\Phi.$ Substituting this into the equation simplifies it to the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+AΦu(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t) & =f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\Phi$ is invertible, we can pre-multiply both sides by $\\Phi^{-1}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate both sides of the final equations (ignoring the constant term):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \\Phi \\displaystyle\\int \\Phi^{-1}\\mathbf{f}(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "99630576-f78f-593a-af83-87c98421e2c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14396 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "94760db8-7585-5363-8838-c0f44735b806" :step/math-academy-id 592305 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166524 :question/id #uuid "6060e2fe-05b3-587d-b5dd-3cdab705eb05" :question/math-academy-id "e-21546"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c20cd9a-5734-5129-93db-55f6dc985b3a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050707}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552459}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Formulas for Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "4a088561-5bc3-556f-b5d5-470901c2c1cf" :step/math-academy-id 592306 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166525 :question/id #uuid "1d203940-c05d-5f30-a8ad-c618d6d6ba5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21547"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050101}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531573}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "387d38d2-d1da-59ba-b0d2-529bed76253e" :step/math-academy-id 592309 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166938 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166526 :question/id #uuid "ce663953-3e5e-55e2-a8db-6d7a531496cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515600}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "9a3cf6ac-30bf-5301-9c2b-c2870c934a65" :step/math-academy-id 592312}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166985}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497449} :activity/id #uuid "b003c2d7-68ae-5238-bc0f-46f982a5621d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e43401-278c-58f1-b818-fad8546bda0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303797}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303797} :answer-field/id #uuid "295a6ea8-2810-531b-90a1-1e521e6ec6e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596f4cf7-f992-59e0-a1ab-314f9b22d6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303002} {:answer/id #uuid "5e995f6d-38bc-503f-add8-819490663c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496865} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a0f445-2c98-5d5e-9321-6d979dc294b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc4c9e61-81e1-5e97-95cb-a89bd2a71a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-330899" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{-t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-6{e}^{2t})-5{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{2t} & -5{e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}12{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 12{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & -6{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{t} \\\\ 9{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "263c0b72-c299-51ac-98e0-f88dd4d823d3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08055b09-dc8e-5e64-a169-6b8646c97e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18t-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302508} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e093a4e-16c7-597e-9369-a2860151d355" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6dc5997-1d4e-5312-9245-798d648973c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-329817" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}12t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-12t-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}12t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 8t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-4t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 8t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 8t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}+\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}+{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=8t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=8\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -4t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =8t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot 8\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}+\\int 8\\,{e}^{-t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t{e}^{-t}-8{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-t}(1+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ -8{e}^{-t}(1+t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t)-16(1+t) \\\\ 2(1-2t)-8(1+t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18t-15 \\\\ -12t-6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7c983da7-ca72-5a73-bf3a-e7f56ec9519d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff2cccf5-489b-5e70-b3a9-9d2661562299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365794} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7d0200-75d6-56dc-9a3b-832b6fae8337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2e^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496829}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496829} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef783e3c-2210-5313-90c8-cc217b3c7be3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "974ddd49-be50-5576-a845-339cab6a2a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365796} {:answer/id #uuid "0aacdf65-443f-56da-b835-155b66a57197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496676} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4dc56ff-d11b-5257-828e-dfd7f038bf79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cab04d20-21da-5031-a5e8-e08eadd456c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336698" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2{e}^{t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{3}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"60px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}-(-2{e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -2{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "842ea0d1-eabb-5f2a-bca0-30802bda115c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee041e3a-2f24-52a5-bb75-0aca1b12f5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497167}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497167} :answer-field/id #uuid "257f025c-7aa8-5698-b6e8-90af2e776c37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497232} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5163d8a-75af-5737-80f1-4aea32343cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497060}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497060} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e897fb2-9841-5b9d-a780-0a38ba920dc8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3cbf4a14-013f-525c-b169-07b2a7a14f29" :question/math-academy-id "q-337102" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2{e}^{3t}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{10}{3}$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}{e}^{t}-0\\cdot {e}^{t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 4{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -2{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 4{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-2}{3}{e}^{3t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-2}{3}{e}^{3t} \\\\ -4{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "98dc1837-552f-563e-a960-0f664d971bb5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4c6396-6267-59ce-81e1-3b70290d6465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80t+50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496717} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bbf6a85-16be-5ce4-a166-d683ed32df25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8720bb82-7e04-58ea-82b1-0eeb9a80a59b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337285" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -6 \\\\ 9 & -8\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$90t+45$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 3{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 15t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-5t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 15t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -5t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 15t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-5t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-5\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -5t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-5t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-5\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{5}{2}\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=15t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=15\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 15t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =15t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot 15\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-15t{e}^{-t}+\\int 15{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-15t{e}^{-t}-15{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =-15{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ 3{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ -15{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}10(1-2t)-60(t+1) \\\\ 15(1-2t)-60(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}80t+50 \\\\ 90t+45\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc7dda2f-cd2a-50cf-8f0a-dcfa59501811"}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496656}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515624} :activity/id #uuid "14db1be8-51e1-5036-b934-216c3bf6e275" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33978283-6287-570d-93ab-bb7631c431be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514988} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5413a60-3a3a-5440-92ee-5f361e71fff8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca58d00f-a386-5b1e-a85b-e37b24ea3447" :question/math-academy-id "q-337284" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3(t-1)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}3t{e}^{3t} \\\\ t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}(3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{3t}-\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=1\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =t\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{t}-{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3t-1 \\\\ 3(t-1)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0418a620-c5b3-5d53-82c0-63ca1c0bfbbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03391546-51e2-592b-b33e-96a0e7dadad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308770}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308770} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4cc6a92-1dbb-533a-bde1-5f5375f2fb88" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c40bb4a2-ee82-5c62-8098-95674097c15b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310321} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3b02da-876b-5e0a-8cf7-c53cb24cd812" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ea97844-39dd-5c98-b9ff-e503c4be0f51" :question/math-academy-id "q-336699" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $5{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-({e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 5{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -3{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "082a26b1-f3c0-5b74-a962-43a65356bce3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e08da9-67bd-5515-a054-84a8357eeeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309281} {:answer/id #uuid "534c0f49-500e-5197-9bbd-6d09cb24788b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309380} {:answer/id #uuid "597a5fcb-acf1-507d-a353-70f05f27cf4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309421} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9f5e84-6187-51e3-b1ba-d485dbd83ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310407} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f089efb-a288-5a25-99bb-2f2c35b14bdb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9413d3-2dd0-5b9c-af86-84118da8b49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308828} {:answer/id #uuid "66dea2be-fb2a-5e49-8f24-f5bf098b70b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309537} {:answer/id #uuid "85bc0b4c-26c1-564c-b0cc-0d3faa27a1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding $Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309789} {:answer/id #uuid "96ddbfc5-4828-537e-9f4f-20b74f781e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309537} :answer-field/id #uuid "89eb1346-c61c-5d99-a38f-a8915bf8aeb6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311184} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148cbe9c-5e50-5894-8133-94a7e83eaffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308901} {:answer/id #uuid "aad5e34e-5cb9-538c-896a-149719edd725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310128} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4009cc-2f28-553e-b2cc-74e5a8c34f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310265} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a20eb3-b4e9-5340-8083-468f920be621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310128} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7d6706-c034-5ad7-b9c2-d78eb5a0f383" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311198} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d49db2-b24b-5622-ad80-9b08aa150e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308947} {:answer/id #uuid "45498a31-a0e4-5a19-bbd1-13164db595c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309276} {:answer/id #uuid "633a649e-40b2-5363-8abc-94d62e0cac29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309509} {:answer/id #uuid "674a326d-23d4-5dbf-818a-b08a2b7071fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308947} :answer-field/id #uuid "b44571e6-f5cf-5a50-a359-5da2ad7dfbff" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7cf3081d-968b-5f36-a1e5-bb052c96fbcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-336713" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-4}} into $\\text{L1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Let's substitute ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$ from $\\text{L4}$ into $\\text{L3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+\\cancel{AΦu(t)}=\\cancel{AΦu(t)}+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3}\\text{ and }\\text{L4}$ by substitution.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "1464fa05-0d62-5c01-b491-e56ce5c8e2e8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5b84d3b-c2a0-5c17-a951-7b667223d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4t-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310336} :answer-field/id #uuid "f75ca1ce-9660-5f2b-8e80-e3b967d7673c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a119389-1e86-5899-9ba6-43b2c31d6800" :question/math-academy-id "q-337288" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $6t-6$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-2t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ 3{e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}(1-2t)+6(t-1) \\\\ 6(t-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4t-5 \\\\ 6t-6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4f772438-7511-5d11-8498-5f7b2fa9adee"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e50ee6-2518-5d34-a3e2-4799e3d17703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309180} :answer-field/id #uuid "573e1e64-9846-5e0e-bb82-38a0518ddaaf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7781a288-b83b-5516-8d03-ce94842577fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309684} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a42318d-7765-5b03-b495-8b35312f578c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6ac8d1ba-163b-54eb-addd-dba5b1956964" :question/math-academy-id "q-337280" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}$ |  |  | $\\frac{17}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-3{e}^{4t})-3{e}^{4t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 5{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c1f84e26-b520-572d-b1af-861828868756" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515622}}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514852}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531717} :activity/id #uuid "ad2891ee-0030-5652-a668-6f7d9a8b1217" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365800 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6418cc68-5c14-5b79-a76b-bd6a187118df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22t-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365802} :answer-field/id #uuid "108a4f0f-4662-57f5-8305-95c9666f98fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fa173e6f-437b-589d-80f8-b753b282ebf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-337219" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $8t-14$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{-t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & -2{e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int t{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=1\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(t)\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2})\\cdot (1\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{t{e}^{-2t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4}(2t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ -\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{4}(2t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12(t-1)-2(2t+1) \\\\ 24(t-1)-(2t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}8t-14 \\\\ 22t-25\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "25f637fb-d499-5964-98b0-d92aa3739826" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91ab3da8-8b65-5c7c-b413-f64ac221623d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531178} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dd0ba3-3b82-5d28-920c-a872d1b66617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531213} {:answer/id #uuid "a66e5a36-33aa-57dd-9127-3bf14e2a3526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531223} {:answer/id #uuid "bc060e7c-ade5-5987-bfcc-22a3b89b7c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531178} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7d19aee-5b0e-5d1e-a94c-b57fbd271c20" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531519} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06460bbd-b07c-5c78-a2a2-80d558e6fce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "initial system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530872} {:answer/id #uuid "0880df10-3963-5d13-be2c-f4f7b1243830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "line $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530880} {:answer/id #uuid "1e95d564-42f5-5e8c-87b1-05cb232304bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "solution of the system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530921} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1e7de1-308b-5df0-8d07-32baa3c11686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "first shifting theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531380}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530872} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfeef9c0-222d-580e-9176-2a58212a74b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531536} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc6ec5e-e5a6-5494-ba80-6ac0d18a888e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531029} {:answer/id #uuid "50c6f9fd-4090-51e9-b85f-8edff140d4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531039} {:answer/id #uuid "82bcbc16-1b95-5f7c-ade5-752010bacc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531149} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7bc21d-2613-5254-8ca6-da3ab5ba690a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531317} :answer-field/id #uuid "e749236f-06ab-5ec0-b55c-1658240128bd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59d9272b-7eb2-549a-868b-86d14c7fdef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336703" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Let's substitute ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$ from $\\text{L1}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into $\\text{initial system of ODEs}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{pre-multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cb68189-e6bd-50df-bf7a-85be4d333c8d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d66787-66e5-569a-91da-fb16e858ec7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309980} :answer-field/id #uuid "33380138-39c7-5cc1-be02-40c0352385d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310928} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57fa1d40-985b-5dc8-bff3-11325100380c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309411} :answer-field/id #uuid "74228cec-035c-567c-bcee-7a90efcf66d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c98926c-5044-5e94-be2a-fc638fdc3f93" :question/math-academy-id "q-337230" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $2{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}-0\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -8{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3431c228-5dc1-5a6b-90ef-eb4019f30372" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca5fb1c3-f4d9-5a37-a9bd-6708fe402752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365810} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af25afe-e912-5735-b096-591cb5639115" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9269cd51-19e6-5e98-96ab-0e051bb84772" :question/math-academy-id "q-337290" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | $-2t-3$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-(-{e}^{t})\\cdot 2{e}^{-2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int \\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{4}{3}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4}{3}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}t{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t}(2t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{8}{3}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{8}{3}t\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{8}{3}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{2t}(2t-1) \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1)-8(t+1) \\\\ 2(2t-1)+8(t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2t-3 \\\\ 4t+2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c5d20bf1-c67d-533b-b7e7-2dfd3e6fc008"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8535759b-fe73-52af-853f-f3486f41d48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531153} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ab610c-28ab-5d09-83f7-d68b274b353d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f89927db-d31d-51e0-b063-5d1318a6634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531369} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa355cf-d45f-5b54-bcec-6c9a1e489b0d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "29e3e5d7-28ea-5e8a-b874-caa92a5ebba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336597" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{-4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{4}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4t}\\cdot (-2{e}^{2t})-0\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{-6t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cef5da74-9e1b-5ac0-acde-a39773d01fea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531679}}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530861}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543662} :activity/id #uuid "4de652dc-8dfe-5cf9-94a7-2a0b45eb733b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/id #uuid "41fc66cd-f988-5c35-8b92-ae13cc6708fa" :topic/math-academy-id 3187} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0379b0b5-fa6e-560f-a805-6be1158baa43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542648} {:answer/id #uuid "735eed43-4b31-5d90-9a34-2ca221059a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542942} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8bf55a-f058-5fdd-972f-dc333a5b367f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543233} {:answer/id #uuid "ec42e44d-b24c-5c90-a2bd-c598ae24cdcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543233} :answer-field/id #uuid "066bec05-268d-5397-834c-3e44e338208b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1db908-97ee-5397-98ed-7bc3e89595d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542758} {:answer/id #uuid "300088b7-6663-5c6a-a630-49ba6f44fea7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542767} {:answer/id #uuid "7bfd1fe4-3d5d-5bb9-a970-ce9cfbca6ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542971} {:answer/id #uuid "f98dee2c-62f5-5811-83f2-92bf71d5fa4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding $Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542971} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a63bbb4-ea88-5980-8688-35e082862fe9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543304} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2c346aa-d8f9-5ec5-926e-8e501ea3de8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543068} {:answer/id #uuid "a528590d-0de2-53bb-88e9-08d39c55baa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543074} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd7240e-e811-561a-b88b-254e37738ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543144} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e27f42-6f5c-5db9-9572-1afe2f03fa55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543144} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1d82869-ee08-5b8e-bf00-ce54c646f823" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002d3a8f-1874-505a-bf0c-1a883637bd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542634} {:answer/id #uuid "273d4cd2-09eb-568f-b28d-d237b7057b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542740} {:answer/id #uuid "b07c1742-57f9-5140-847d-5f008bfe2c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543100} {:answer/id #uuid "bea06e41-a0fd-5f64-81ed-4cbc5f7f834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542634} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffeb5c19-09f0-5528-a066-b2ec8f17f60c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41ff18d2-5493-53b7-9e77-82f1434ea309" :question/math-academy-id "q-336712" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}} matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "060efb46-b822-5dba-9e37-6c83cd83dada" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d356bc26-57d5-59ea-80f6-ad3d535a1609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36t-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310448} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e3e1f1-67f2-5abf-8891-7159f370a35a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ed0b294-0a14-518d-b751-a90930d00a2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337287" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | $30t-26$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-t}\\cdot 3{e}^{3t}-{e}^{3t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2t}}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}3t{e}^{t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})-\\int (-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t})\\cdot (-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{3}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3{e}^{t}(t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}27(t-1)+(3t+1) \\\\ 27(t-1)+3(3t+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}30t-26 \\\\ 36t-24\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "129dd3b0-de6d-5f1e-9fbb-5a9a8c1efa71"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1325a0fc-b13c-580f-a7f6-a3a51f300330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542688} {:answer/id #uuid "52f9ebdd-9edc-54cd-a07f-f252940848a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542863} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb8056e-d2ee-54d2-acd4-798a4269834f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543177} {:answer/id #uuid "e72d2758-3f09-53d5-8aa4-e20cee50d05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542863} :answer-field/id #uuid "46cc18a9-7bbf-5c79-9620-7ec7f585a7f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543335} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e015a1-706f-5177-93db-0bf6a2d93adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542683} {:answer/id #uuid "6649e667-7698-5ea3-9463-8604cfe2c33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542914} {:answer/id #uuid "e48c8dad-ac17-5754-a5c4-9a6aca499939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543219} {:answer/id #uuid "ec66c7ac-63e5-5bbf-a968-e25b5488437a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542683} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b0d288-93b4-5bf0-996b-46ae4e23d975" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543369} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6171db-4601-5d55-99e7-40ac8d10f6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542661} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1fcebf-64ee-5283-aebc-5134bcebf561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542707} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0d3c68-e600-5772-be76-16d671d4234f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542811} {:answer/id #uuid "4692373c-5bda-5889-b3cb-6f17f3ab4956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542830} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a30296a-81a8-5e8f-8848-aa76261f03d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "026f1da3-fe0b-5238-91da-8230388ad940" :question/math-academy-id "q-336702" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-2}} by substitution.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into $\\text{L1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ To get $\\text{L2},$ we are $\\text{differentiating}$ with respect to $t$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φu(t))^{′} \\\\ {x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ Let's substitute ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$ from $\\text{L4}$ into $\\text{L3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+\\cancel{AΦu(t)}=\\cancel{AΦu(t)}+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3}\\text{ and }\\text{L4}$ by substitution.\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1}.$"} :step/id #uuid "2c2e7d30-c6f5-5cad-87f1-8742a876ba3d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543621}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b41ef18b-4fd8-5f85-9118-8892db11b4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "line $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396953} {:answer/id #uuid "d93b8a1a-4307-506a-b249-296d73fda268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "initial system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396954} {:answer/id #uuid "69db23bb-9f00-5ed5-a314-a4398d8cb228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "solution of the system of ODEs" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396955} {:answer/id #uuid "3a963511-ca8a-5ebc-85ff-04e4527811af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "first shifting theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396954} :answer-field/id #uuid "15760b45-942f-5371-a4af-6567ed71b662" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73fc77df-9183-5392-9f5f-84c6b7225cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396958} {:answer/id #uuid "33ab2692-3bf2-5c51-81e7-2d7540647496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396959} {:answer/id #uuid "7b292260-1158-5bf9-8ff9-6e83083ce40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396960} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5ac2ca-d399-5fa8-b244-bbd050e7ea77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396958} :answer-field/id #uuid "369c338a-25f1-56f5-856e-c6afcea5d80e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aa0013e-2425-55c4-9d94-e57a745924bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396963} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4fa214-db95-5f01-97b9-814c953c2a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396964} {:answer/id #uuid "722383ab-f057-5be9-88d9-1807d49eabbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396965} {:answer/id #uuid "96b39a06-7a60-5b50-821a-8591067e9eef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396966} :answer-field/id #uuid "65afa9b7-d90a-53f3-b255-4185b375d4c8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dd43670-1646-534f-9b82-0ec4c2ab4066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "adding $Φ$ to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396968} {:answer/id #uuid "fabeb164-e1c7-5722-8dd4-7476a0c9cdb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396969} {:answer/id #uuid "0314521e-1ee3-553c-a49a-28490b5dc131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396970} {:answer/id #uuid "b70f4cdd-82f4-5102-b82d-3bb67d075165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "pre-multiplying by ${Φ}^{-1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396970} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd7b65f1-ae60-595f-b010-b4d039e76cf4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f3e98464-6e85-513e-a930-d0947c1c72b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336711" :question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that {{field-4}} matrix." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ To get $\\text{L2},$ we are $\\text{differentiating}$ with respect to $t$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φu(t))^{′} \\\\ {x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Let's substitute ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$ from $\\text{L1}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ into the initial system of ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t)+f(t) \\\\ Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t) & =AΦu(t)+f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ into $\\text{initial system of ODEs}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are linearly independent. So, $Φ$ is invertible. Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{pre-multiplying by }{Φ}^{-1}$ both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\cdot Φ{u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ ({Φ}^{-1}Φ)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={Φ}^{-1}\\cdot f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ i.e., by substituting any column $c(t)$ of $Φ$ into the homogeneous system, we get\n\n$$\n{c}^{′}(t)=Ac(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}^{′}(t)$ is the corresponding column of derivatives in $Φ.$ As a result, this means that ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from the fact that $Φ\\text{ is a fundamental}$ matrix."} :step/id #uuid "3570a941-938a-5632-bc18-ea2f8f3074a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afb4a52-a6fa-5628-a987-67c61d0437bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a fundamental" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542664} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ca98ef-d9ea-5cb8-842b-28069c76b8c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is an invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543087} {:answer/id #uuid "b822b7c3-4f8f-510d-ab76-abeacd9652bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is the identity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543120} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f41f68-2c43-533b-994f-b50e5699aa55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Φ$ is a square" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542664} :answer-field/id #uuid "86ebf5a0-9610-584a-85e1-88d2ed6cfed3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543382} {:answer-field/ch